The user wants a light theme, silver/white based, with red and green blended in buttons, graphs etc. Not too much green. So I need to rework the theme.

Currently default is dark with green brand. I need to:

Switch default to light theme (data-bs-theme="light").
Silver/white palette: cool greys, subtle silver gradients, slate text, white surfaces with fine borders, maybe subtle metallic sheen.
Accent colors: red (#d62828-ish / crimson) and green (#157347 / emerald) used in buttons, charts, tags. Secondary neutral accents: slate/graphite, maybe steel blue for variety in charts — but the user said blend of red and green in buttons, graphs. So charts should use red/green palette plus greys/silvers.
Keep dark mode toggle available (still functional) but light is the default and the polished one.
Typography: pair a distinctive display face with readable body face. Currently Inter + JetBrains Mono. I should add a display face — something distinctive but appropriate for a banking admin. Maybe "Sora" or "Archivo" or "Manrope" for display, with "Inter" as body? Inter alone is discouraged as single-family. Let's use display: "Sora" (geometric, distinctive) or "Archivo" — for a fintech admin, "Sora" or "Space Grotesk" works. Body: "IBM Plex Sans" or "Inter". Mono: "JetBrains Mono" (good for tabular figures).
I'll go: Display = "Sora" (weights 600-800), Body = "Inter" (400-600), Mono = "JetBrains Mono". Hmm, "avoid single-family Inter typography" — pairing Sora display + Inter body is fine since it's a pairing.

Actually, let me choose something a bit more distinctive: "Instrument Sans"? "Archivo"? For a Kenyan digital bank admin, "Sora" is nice and modern. I'll use Sora for display headings/hero numbers and Inter for UI body text. Plus JetBrains Mono for figures.

Now, redesign details:

Silver/white: background #f4f6f9 with a very subtle brushed-silver gradient; surfaces pure white; borders #e3e8ef / #d7dee8; text #14181f slate.
Silver metallic accents: linear-gradient(180deg,#fff,#f7f9fb) on cards headers; subtle inset highlight box-shadow: inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,.9).
Sidebar: instead of dark navy, make it silver-white too? A light silver sidebar with red/green accents would fit "silver white theme". Yes — light sidebar: white surface, right border, nav items in slate with hover wash, active item with red/green? Let's use a graphite-to-silver gradient? Better: light sidebar (#fbfcfd) with a subtle silver sheen, active state = white with left accent bar in emerald (or a red/green gradient). Keep it silver/white so the whole app is cohesive.
Actually to give it character and avoid "flat static", I'll keep the sidebar a slightly cooler silver (#f6f8fa) with a fine vertical silver sheen and the active item having a white pill with a subtle shadow and a red→green gradient left bar. That's distinctive.

Hero card: currently dark green gradient. Change to a silver "brushed metal" panel: white → silver gradient with a red/green duo accent, big display numbers in graphite. Add subtle guilloche/hairline pattern (security-print feel, characteristic of banking). Keep it light: linear-gradient(135deg,#ffffff 0%, #f2f5f8 45%, #e8edf3 100%) with fine diagonal hairlines and a thin red→green top border. Metric tiles = white with hairline borders.
Hmm, but the hero needs contrast. Strong contrast in type sizes: the headline "KES 2.47B" in Sora 800 at large size, graphite #0f141b, with a green delta chip and red for negative. Good.

Charts: recharts colors → use palette: emerald #157f52/#2f9e6f, red #c81e3c/#e23a4f, plus silver/greys #94a3b8, #64748b, and steel #5b7c99? The user said blend of red and green in graphs. I'll use a palette of: #0f9d58-ish emerald, #c62828-ish red, and neutral silvers/graphite plus a muted slate-blue for one or two series for legibility. Keep revenue breakdown slices mostly red/green/silver variations? That could look muddy with 7 slices. I'll define a palette array: emerald, crimson, slate, amber-grey, teal-grey, rose, graphite. Mostly desaturated silver-greys with red and green as the anchor colors.
