Buttons: .btn-pm should be... The user says "blend of red and green in buttons". So: primary action = green (emerald) for confirm/success, danger actions = red. And a "blend" — maybe a split gradient button for the primary CTA: gradient from crimson to emerald would look odd. Better: define two solid button variants: .btn-pm (graphite/silver for neutral primary) and .btn-pm-success (emerald), .btn-pm-danger (crimson). And a distinctive "blend" accent used sparingly: e.g., the primary CTA gets a white-on-graphite look with a red→green gradient underline/glow. Hmm.
Simplest and tasteful:

.btn-pm = graphite (deep slate #1c2530) with white text — strong contrast on silver.
.btn-pm-emerald / reuse btn-success-like .btn-pm-ok = emerald gradient.
.btn-pm-danger = crimson gradient.
.btn-pm-outline = silver. And the "blend" appears in: the hero top hairline (red→green gradient rule), session timer bar (red→green? no, that's weird), the active nav bar (red→green gradient), section index badges, focus rings, and the progress bars. That gives the blend without garish buttons.
Actually the user explicitly says "let it be silver white theme with blend of red and green in buttons graghs etc". So buttons should use red and green. I'll make the primary CTA green, danger red, and neutral silver/graphite. And keep a small red→green gradient element for identity.

Also need: living elements, motion, scroll reveals, micro-interactions. Add scroll-reveal (IntersectionObserver) for sections, count-up animation on hero numbers, hover lifts (already), animated live pulse, ambient layered background (silver mesh + fine diagonal guilloche lines + soft radial washes in red/green at very low opacity).
Let me add a useCountUp hook for hero values and section reveal component. That makes it feel alive.

